## Tracker

Split by the author on 2026-11-10 into two merge requests, one raid each:

- Ticket 1508: the Codex reader, the per-runtime seam and the per-runtime
  reporting (first wave).
- Ticket 1509: the Pi reader on that seam, blocked by 1508 (second raid,
  executes after 1508 merges: both edit `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`).

Partition boundary: 1508 owns everything the two runtimes share (the seam,
the runtimes-read report, could-not-look and the exit code); 1509 adds one
reader and its entry. This tracker closes after both children have merged and
the integration review has run the audit on the three live corpora; the exit
criteria below are that review's checklist.

## Context

Ticket 1499 (PR 2391, merged 2026-11-10) made `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`
answer "which sessions overran their context ceiling". It walks
`~/.claude/projects` and parses Claude Code's transcript format only. The
design note `design/2026-11-09-context-length-strategy.md` deferred "the same
reading on Codex and Pi" with no ticket until the author opened it; the author
opened it on 2026-11-10, after asking whether PR 2391 had considered other
runtimes.

The shared rules bind every runtime to the same ceilings
(`skills/route/ceilings.json` already names Sol and Astra), so a count that
leaves two runtimes out is not a count of the harness's sessions.

Observed on padme, 2026-11-10 (key names only, no transcript content read):

- Codex: 358 `rollout-*.jsonl` files under `~/.codex/sessions` modified since
  2026-11-01. Each turn has an `event_msg` record of type `token_count`
  carrying `payload.info.last_token_usage` with `input_tokens`,
  `cached_input_tokens`, `cache_write_input_tokens` and `total_tokens`. Model
  ids seen in one file: `gpt-6-astra`, `gpt-6-luna`, `gpt-6.1-sol`.
- Pi: 78 `*.jsonl` files under `~/.pi/agent/sessions`, the newest dated
  2026-11-01. Assistant records carry `message.usage` with `input`, `output`,
  `cacheRead`, `cacheWrite` and `totalTokens`; a `model_change` record names
  the model.

Not established: whether Codex's `input_tokens` already includes
`cached_input_tokens`. Settle it on one transcript before summing; a double
count would put every Codex session over its ceiling.

## Scope

- A reader per runtime behind the audit's existing session trajectory, same
  definition of context length as ticket 1499: the whole prompt of a turn.
- The audit's table and JSON say which runtimes were read and count sessions
  per runtime. An interim line stating "Claude Code transcripts only" lands
  separately; this ticket replaces it.
- A runtime whose corpus is absent or unreadable is reported as could not
  look, never as zero sessions.

Out of scope: a status line or any live display on Codex or Pi (each runtime
has its own surface); compaction and missed-run cost on the other runtimes
(their compaction records and prices differ, a later ticket if the counts
call for it); ceilings for local models served to Pi.

## Test

On fixture transcripts, one per runtime: a session past its model's ceiling
is counted under its runtime; a turn outside the window is not counted; an
absent corpus for one runtime is reported as could not look while the others
are still counted.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The audit counts Codex and Pi main sessions beside Claude Code's, with
  the whole-prompt definition, and its output names each runtime read
- [ ] A runtime it could not read is reported as such, with a non-zero exit
  only when no corpus at all is readable
- [ ] The Codex `input_tokens` question above is answered in the reader's
  docstring, with the transcript evidence
- [ ] `make check` passes
